Better design: negative control should assert that the mutation *detection* is specific: for each mutant, all checks other than the mutated/affected one remain passing. Actually I already record `checks_failing`. To prove no false positives, I can use a "repair-normalized" clean bundle: apply an in-memory correction of the known real drift (restore declared pin to measured bytes) and confirm all checks pass on that normalized copy. That's a legitimate control: it shows the checker passes when the condition holds. I'll add: negative control = normalized copy (FROZEN pin updated to measured for the one drifted file) passes all checks. And separately report the real drift as a hard failure/finding on the actual bundle.

